    title = {Kant on Civil Self-Sufficiency},
    journal = {Archiv für Geschichte der Philosophie},
    year = {2023},
    volume = {105},
    number = {1},
    pages = {118--140},
    doi = {10.1515/agph-2020-0030},
    note = {SUMMARY: Argues that Kant attributes civil self-sufficiency, the qualification for active citizenship and political participation, to those who are not under the authority of any private individual for their survival. The author holds this reading to be more textually grounded than the dominant reading, which understands self-sufficiency in terms of economic relations alone, and that Kant's interest in relations of authority reflects a concern to eliminate certain forms of corruption in public lawgiving (abstract). SUBFIELD AND DEBATE: Political and legal philosophy, Doctrine of Right: the active/passive citizen distinction and the dependence of passive citizens; bears on the debate over Kant's exclusion of dependents from citizenship. SIGNIFICANCE INDICATORS: S2 citations 12 and CrossRef is-referenced-by-count 13 (retrieved 2026-10-01). Venue: Archiv für Geschichte der Philosophie 105(1). YEAR NOTE: CrossRef gives print publication 2023-03-13 and online publication 2023-03-15.},
