    title = {The Origins of Kant's Aesthetics},
    publisher = {Cambridge University Press},
    year = {2023},
    doi = {10.1017/9781009209403},
    note = {SUMMARY: Examines the sources and development of Kant's aesthetics through his publications, correspondence, handwritten notes and university lectures, in eight chapters on aesthetic judgment and normativity, formal beauty, partly conceptual beauty, genius, the fine arts, the sublime, ugliness and disgust, and humor. It considers how Kant's thought was shaped by authors including Wolff, Baumgarten, Meier, Mendelssohn, Sulzer, Herder, Hutcheson, Hume, Burke, Home, Batteux, Rousseau and Voltaire (publisher abstract). SUBFIELD AND DEBATE: Aesthetics with pre-critical and lecture material: the development of Kant's aesthetic theory before and around the third Critique, and its eighteenth-century sources. SIGNIFICANCE INDICATORS: S2 citations 10 and CrossRef is-referenced-by-count 10 (retrieved 2026-10-01). Publisher: Cambridge University Press. Reviews found by CrossRef search: Kantian Review (McQuillan, 2024), Estetika (Erkan, 2025); a Kantian Review discussion piece by Zinkin (2024) also addresses the book. NDPR review: not retrieved. YEAR NOTE: CrossRef gives print publication 2023-01-26 and online publication 2024-01-10.},
